WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:07.581
This is the opening scene from Scream.&nbsp;

00:00:07.581 --> 00:00:10.540
And this is the opening for Scream 2.&nbsp;

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.043
They’re supposed to look almost identical.

00:00:13.043 --> 00:00:15.777
They’re both showing the murder 
of the same character.

00:00:15.777 --> 00:00:21.550
But when you put them next to each other,
the original stab looks way more realistic...

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:24.293
because of the way they use this.&nbsp;

00:00:24.824 --> 00:00:29.247
Horror movie props are carefully designed
to make you think they’re real

00:00:29.247 --> 00:00:31.292
thanks to a little movie magic. &nbsp;

00:00:36.883 --> 00:00:42.253
This is Nate Ragon, a Scream aficionado, who
worked on Scream 4 and 5, and

00:00:42.253 --> 00:00:45.549
who has a bunch of props from the movies.

00:00:45.549 --> 00:00:48.030
RAGON: This is a hero knife from Scream 2.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:49.914
Ghost Face costume from Scream 3.

00:00:49.914 --> 00:00:52.403
Hero knives for Scream 3.

00:00:52.403 --> 00:00:54.330
This is an actual buck knife.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:58.344
For the original Scream, they literally just
took an actual buck knife

00:00:58.344 --> 00:00:59.590
and they dulled it down.

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:02.477
Like they rounded off the tip 
and they dulled it.

00:01:02.477 --> 00:01:08.049
Centuries ago, dulled knives and swords 
and daggers were used in stage duels.&nbsp;

00:01:08.049 --> 00:01:11.424
And that translated pretty seamlessly 
to early adventure films.

00:01:11.424 --> 00:01:15.000
And later to films with violent killers.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.850
Dulled knives work really well onscreen for
“hero shots,”

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.476
or close ups, that sell that 
the knife is real.

00:01:22.476 --> 00:01:28.711
But a dulled knife is still incredibly dangerous,
and safety is paramount on film sets.

00:01:28.711 --> 00:01:33.892
There are tons of rules for using items 
as innocuous as fog machines or make up

00:01:33.892 --> 00:01:36.560
all the way to the most dangerous pyrotechnics.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.712
To avoid accidents, filmmakers often use 
a variety of prop knives for different shots

00:01:41.712 --> 00:01:45.250
then stitch them together into one scene.

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:47.372
RAGON: The plastic ones were 
what they would use

00:01:47.372 --> 00:01:49.385
if it was ever like pointed 
at someone's face.

00:01:49.385 --> 00:01:52.623
Anywhere near an actor's face,
it would either be rubber or plastic.

00:01:52.623 --> 00:01:55.979
RAGON: When the killers like running, he's
probably holding one of the rubber knives

00:01:55.979 --> 00:01:58.069
just in case you know he falls on it.

00:01:58.069 --> 00:02:01.987
And then, of course, you would have like 
the retractable hitting the person.

00:02:01.987 --> 00:02:05.217
RAGON: And then when they pull 
the retractable out, that's another shot.

00:02:05.217 --> 00:02:08.510
And then it would go back to the real knife
in the hand with blood on it.

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:11.171
In a video about the Making Of 
Screams 1 through 3

00:02:11.171 --> 00:02:15.001
the founder of KNB EFX group, 
a special effects company

00:02:15.001 --> 00:02:16.925
had this to say 
about his work

00:02:16.925 --> 00:02:21.124
"This wasn’t a monster movie, it didn’t
have a ton of effects in it." 

00:02:21.124 --> 00:02:25.379
"We had a few old-fashioned gore gags, 
with the collapsable knives.”

00:02:25.379 --> 00:02:28.650
With practical effects, 
less is sometimes more.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:33.510
The reason this shot in Scream 2 looks fake
is because they showcased the wobble

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.903
of an imperfect retractable stab.

00:02:35.903 --> 00:02:37.675
Whereas in the original.

00:02:37.675 --> 00:02:40.721
RAGON: You can tell the glove 
is completely hiding that awkward

00:02:40.721 --> 00:02:42.982
kind of wonky looking handle.

00:02:42.982 --> 00:02:46.902
HART: The thing about the retractable knife
is that it's easy for the blade to bind up.

00:02:46.902 --> 00:02:51.170
And if the blade binds up then all of a sudden
you're stabbing them for real.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:52.400
There's horror stories of some.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.842
There was a theater and then they had retractable
knives and he got hit just below the pads,

00:02:56.842 --> 00:03:01.170
so that actually punctured his lung 
while he was out on stage.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:06.676
Eric Hart is a prop maker and stage props
professor at North Carolina university.

00:03:06.676 --> 00:03:10.690
HART: There's a lot of things we do, we can
do pyrotechnics, we could hang actors.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.123
We could do all sorts of stuff and 
we can make it safe.

00:03:13.123 --> 00:03:16.991
But you can't make a retractable knife safe.

00:03:16.991 --> 00:03:20.999
To avoid these risks, filmmakers might use
digital replacements.

00:03:20.999 --> 00:03:26.519
In this VFX breakdown from Zombieland 2, Emma
Stone is holding just the hilt of a knife.

00:03:26.519 --> 00:03:30.000
The blade and the blood were both 
added in post.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.841
But CG blood isn't always going to 
serve the purpose.

00:03:32.841 --> 00:03:35.654
Sometimes you need the real, fake thing.

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:42.418
HART: With a blood knife, you usually have
a little container of blood.

00:03:42.418 --> 00:03:44.768
If you can, you can hide it inside the handle.

00:03:44.768 --> 00:03:49.849
And then you just have some kind of tube running
down the blade and you put that on the side

00:03:49.849 --> 00:03:53.400
that the audience doesn't see and 
tape it in place.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:58.835
HART: And then as you drag the knife across
whatever you're cutting, you kinda squeeze

00:03:58.835 --> 00:04:03.120
the tube and you time it so that the blood
starts coming out, and then it looks like

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.489
the knife is actually cutting 
into the skin.

00:04:06.278 --> 00:04:12.336
It's simple solution, but in order for it
to look real, it all depends on the blood.

00:04:12.336 --> 00:04:17.627
In the black and white era of Hollywood, Hershey’s
chocolate syrup was a frequent blood stand in

00:04:17.627 --> 00:04:20.603
most famously used in 
Alfred Hitchcock’s Psycho.

00:04:20.603 --> 00:04:26.820
The effect is convincing, but color film 
needed better fake blood.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.249
In early films, blood often looked 
too thin and too bright

00:04:30.249 --> 00:04:33.463
like this scene from the 1958
version of Dracula.

00:04:33.463 --> 00:04:35.946
HART: You know, when you look at blood, 
it's not pure red.

00:04:35.946 --> 00:04:38.080
You need a little bit of 
something else in it.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:42.158
By the 70s, films like Evil Dead and Carrie
were using a type of blood

00:04:42.158 --> 00:04:44.303
called Kensington gore.

00:04:44.303 --> 00:04:50.360
It was made by a British pharmacist, and named
after a street of the same name in London.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:55.919
The recipe included water, food coloring,
and the game changing factor - corn syrup

00:04:55.919 --> 00:04:57.750
- which added thickness.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:02.180
When it's dripping off of you, when it's flowing,
it's flowing the way blood does.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:05.556
HART: It's not dripping like red water.

00:05:05.556 --> 00:05:10.810
Dick Smith, one of the godfathers of film
makeup took this recipe one step further.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:14.703
He added Kodak Photo-Flo, a poisonous 
wetting agent commonly used

00:05:14.703 --> 00:05:16.889
when developing photo negatives.

00:05:16.889 --> 00:05:22.500
This allowed the blood to believably soak
into clothing, while retaining that thickness.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:27.120
For this scene in Scream, you can see the
special effects team coating Drew Barrymore’s

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:30.389
neck and clothes with fake blood, out of a
ketchup squeeze bottle.

00:05:30.389 --> 00:05:33.145
“Oh, did you just get that in my hair?”

00:05:33.145 --> 00:05:38.304
Movies today use variations on all these recipes,
depending on what the scene calls for.

00:05:38.304 --> 00:05:40.699
If it's coming out of a blood knife, 
it's got to flow.

00:05:40.699 --> 00:05:42.139
You can't have, like, really chunky stuff.

00:05:42.139 --> 00:05:45.731
HART: And if they're actually swallowing it,
you want it to be food safe.

00:05:45.731 --> 00:05:50.603
If it's like splattering on carpets, you want 
to be able to wash it out.

00:05:50.603 --> 00:05:54.099
The next time you're watching like a low-budget
horror, check how many times they have

00:05:54.099 --> 00:05:56.846
a really bloody scene in the bathroom, 
like in the shower.

00:05:56.846 --> 00:06:01.242
Because that's really easy for them to clean
after they're done filming.

00:06:01.242 --> 00:06:04.828
But in situations where the blood has to be
more explosive

00:06:04.828 --> 00:06:06.989
filmmakers turn to another prop.

00:06:09.681 --> 00:06:13.954
Squibs are tiny explosives often used for
gunshot scenes.

00:06:13.954 --> 00:06:18.880
Before squibs, filmmakers relied on acting
to sell a bullet hits.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:23.194
This climactic death from the film High Noon,
is a good example of how that...

00:06:23.194 --> 00:06:26.530
doesn’t always look right.

00:06:27.799 --> 00:06:32.927
Here, Roger George Special Effects is demonstrating
how their Hit Kit squibs work.

00:06:32.927 --> 00:06:37.491
Propmasters attach a small explosive to a
container filled with fake blood

00:06:37.491 --> 00:06:41.575
then attach it to the actor, usually with 
something protective underneath.

00:06:41.575 --> 00:06:45.382
A licensed pyrotechnician will detonate it
at the right time, and the blood will seem

00:06:45.382 --> 00:06:48.773
to explode out of you.

00:06:48.773 --> 00:06:52.192
For larger blood spurts, like these 
ridiculous explosions

00:06:52.192 --> 00:06:54.489
at the end of the campy movie 
Ready or Not 

00:06:54.489 --> 00:06:56.669
filmmakers turn 
to something else.

00:06:56.669 --> 00:06:59.849
And that, I do have it. I got one of the 
blood cannons I built.

00:06:59.849 --> 00:07:03.669
It's the same as a confetti cannon, 
but with blood.

00:07:03.669 --> 00:07:08.729
For the bigger squirts, like this one in the
Babysitter, films will often use air pressure

00:07:08.729 --> 00:07:10.790
to spray blood continuously.

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:15.146
If it's hitting an artery, then it would be
like pulsating out.

00:07:15.146 --> 00:07:18.281
So you have somebody like just off camera
who's able to sort of

00:07:18.281 --> 00:07:20.760
work the pump and the pressure.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:26.050
HART: With air, if you just need a slowly
appearing pool of blood underneath the body,

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:31.786
you could just have like, one PSI of pressure
just slowly pushing that blood out.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:39.240
Prop knives and blood are only one part 
of making movies look real.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:41.983
The rest of the work is often hidden.

00:07:41.983 --> 00:07:45.875
After all, it’s what you don’t see 
that makes the difference.

00:07:45.875 --> 00:07:51.250
Ghostface’s most brutal attacks are often
slightly behind objects, covered by hands,

00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:53.465
or just out of frame.

00:07:53.465 --> 00:07:58.794
The killer in Psycho almost never touches
Marion Cranes actual body.

00:07:58.794 --> 00:08:01.893
And Michael Meyers kills people offscreen.

00:08:01.893 --> 00:08:05.562
But the blood, guts, and sound effects
pull the trick on us

00:08:05.562 --> 00:08:09.118
making us feel like we’ve seen it.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:12.629
Thanks so much for watching!

00:08:12.629 --> 00:08:15.937
We set out a few weeks ago to make a video
about how horror movies

00:08:15.937 --> 00:08:18.084
make fake violence look real.

00:08:18.084 --> 00:08:23.680
And while I was editing this piece, the importance
of prop safety was highlighted by some really

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:25.270
devastating news.

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:30.303
The tragic death of DP Halyna Hutchins 
on the set of the film Rust.

00:08:30.303 --> 00:08:34.880
When watching movies, it can be really easy
to forget how high the stakes are for the

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:36.560
people involved.

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:40.679
While prop accidents are uncommon, 
they do happen.

00:08:40.679 --> 00:08:45.000
A similar incident occurred in 1993 
on the set of The Crow.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:50.520
We don’t talk about firearm props in the piece, 
but the LA Times has a thorough explainer

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:55.410
on how prop guns work — which provides some
clarity about “how a blank cartridge might

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:58.720
have ended up propelling projectiles” on
the set of The Crow.

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:02.770
The topic can get really deep, so If you’re
interested in reading more

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:04.630
we’ll leave some links in the description below.

